# Three statement forms from one sweep
# (bug-nilpy-chained-assign-power-assign-and-semicolon-statements). The
# semicolon form already worked by the time this was picked up and is pinned
# here so it stays working.
#
# 1. CHAINED ASSIGNMENT `a = b = 5`. Python binds every target to ONE evaluation
#    of the right-hand side, so `p = q = f()` calls f ONCE — the `calls` row is
#    what pins that, and it is why this cannot desugar to `q = f(); p = f()`.
#    Assignment is left to right through a hidden temp. PLAIN NAME targets only;
#    `d[k] = d2[k2] = f()` is refused by name rather than half-lowered.
#    It used to report "undefined variable (b)", naming the wrong thing entirely.
#
# 2. `**=`. `**` is not a binop TOKEN — it lowers through pypow_v and the
#    __pow__/__rpow__ dunders — so `**=` lexes as its own token (tkPowEq,
#    APPENDED to the enum, never inserted) and desugars through the same
#    PyMakePow the expression form uses. A second hand-built pypow_v call would
#    have silently dropped the dunder dispatch.
#    The RESULT WIDENS: `2 ** 3` is an int but `2 ** -1` is a FLOAT, so the
#    target must not stay an int. Inside a def the trial parse notes that from
#    the lowered node; at MODULE scope it needs a token-shape arm, exactly as
#    `/=` did before it.
#
# Every expectation is CPython's.

a = b = 5
print(a, b)

x = y = z = "hi"
print(x, y, z)

calls = []
def f():
    calls.append(1)
    return 7

p = q = f()
print(p, q, len(calls))          # ONE call

def g():
    m = n = 3
    return m + n
print(g())

# both names bind the SAME object
lst = lst2 = [1, 2]
lst.append(3)
print(lst, lst2)

# the right-hand side is evaluated before any target is stored
i = 1
i = j = i + 1
print(i, j)

# **= at module scope: int result, float result, float exponent
pw = 2
pw **= 3
print(pw)
neg = 2
neg **= -1
print(neg)
rt = 4
rt **= 0.5
print(rt)

# ...and inside a def
def h():
    v = 2
    v **= -1
    return v
def k2():
    w = 4
    w **= 0.5
    return w
print(h(), k2())

# semicolon-separated statements
s = 5
s += 1; s -= 2
print(s)
